Binary search works whenever you can ask a **yes/no question** whose answer flips exactly once as you move along the data.

## The loop

- Keep a range `lo..hi` that still might contain the answer.
- Look at the middle. The answer tells you which half to throw away.
- Stop when the range is one element.

```cpp
int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
while (lo < hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (ok(mid)) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
}
```

## Why `lo + (hi - lo) / 2`

1. `lo + hi` can overflow a 32-bit integer.
2. The subtraction form cannot.

solution.cpp:
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int lowerBound(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (nums[mid] >= target) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
};
